const timelineEvents = [
  { year: '1876',   label: 'Japan forces Korea to open its ports via unequal treaty.' },
  { year: '1905',   label: 'Korea becomes a Japanese protectorate after Russo-Japanese War.' },
  { year: '1910',   label: 'Japan formally annexes Korea. The novel starts here.', highlight: true },
  { year: '1919',   label: 'March First Movement. Two million protesters; 7,500 killed.', highlight: true },
  { year: '1923',   label: 'Great Kantō Earthquake. Japanese vigilantes massacre thousands of Koreans.' },
  { year: '1930s',  label: "Sunja and Isak arrive in Osaka's Ikaino neighborhood.", highlight: true },
  { year: '1940',   label: 'Sōshi-kaimei: Koreans forced to register Japanese names.', highlight: true },
  { year: '1945',   label: 'Japan surrenders. ~600,000 Koreans remain in Japan.' },
  { year: '1952',   label: 'San Francisco Treaty: Zainichi Koreans stripped of Japanese nationality.', highlight: true },
  { year: '1965',   label: 'Japan–Korea normalization. Limited special permanent residency granted.' },
  { year: '1989',   label: "The novel ends here — Tokyo at the bubble economy's peak.", highlight: true },
];
---

<SummerLayout
  title="The World of the Novel — Pachinko"
  section="pachinko"
  spoke="world"
>
  <div class="sr-spoke-header">
    <span class="sr-spoke-header-eyebrow">Pachinko  ·  Spoke 01 of 05</span>
    <h1 class="sr-spoke-header-title">The World of the Novel</h1>
    <p class="sr-spoke-header-deck">Yeongdo to Tokyo across eighty years — the map of the family's world, 1910 to 1989.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="sr-spoke-layout">
    <div class="sr-spoke-prose">

      <p class="sr-lede"><em>Pachinko</em> spans eighty years and four generations. It begins in 1910 — the year Japan annexed Korea — and ends in 1989, at the peak of Japan's economic boom. A family that starts in a Korean fishing village ends in a Tokyo skyscraper.</p>

      <h2>Where the Novel Begins: Yeongdo, Busan</h2>

      <p>The novel opens in Yeongdo, a small island attached to the port city of Busan on Korea's southeastern coast, the closest point on the peninsula to Japan. In 1910, Yeongdo was a fishing community: tight, poor, and largely self-sufficient. Sunja's family runs a boarding house there, feeding and sheltering the men who pass through.</p>

      <p>Busan matters geographically because it is the city Koreans crossed from when they came to Japan. The ferry route from Busan to Shimonoseki — a passage of roughly 200 kilometers across the Korea Strait — opened in 1905 and became one of the most traveled corridors in twentieth-century Asia, carrying colonized workers, wartime conscripts, refugees, and, eventually, families making a life they had not planned. Additional routes followed: Jeju to Osaka in 1923, Yeosu to Shimonoseki in 1931.</p>

      <p>The volume was enormous. By the late 1920s and early 1930s, between 80,000 and 150,000 Koreans were crossing to Japan each year; in 1930 alone, 400,000 crossings were recorded. The colonial government imposed inspection requirements before boarding: travelers had to demonstrate at least 10 yen in funds, some Japanese-language ability, and confirmed employment waiting on the other side. These requirements filtered the crossings without stopping them. The conditions that made staying in Korea impossible were also the conditions that made meeting the requirements difficult.</p>

      <ImageCaption
        src="/images/summer-reading/pachinko/p1-yeongdo-1921.jpg"
        alt="Waterfront view of Busan (Fusan), 1921, showing docks and buildings along the harbor"
        figureNumber={1}
        caption="Busan (Fusan) waterfront, 1921. The Korea Strait crossing from this harbor to Shimonoseki, Japan, was one of the most traveled maritime corridors of the twentieth century."
        credit="Chōsen Shashinchō [Photographic Album of Korea], 1921. Public domain."
        accent="pachinko"
      />

      <p>The Korea Sunja's family inhabits is already a colonized country. Japan annexed Korea in 1910, the year the novel begins, and the world the first generation inhabits is one shaped entirely by that fact: land surveyed and redistributed, languages suppressed, names under pressure, the basic economic conditions of Korean rural life reorganized to serve Japanese interests. Sunja's poverty is the product of a specific historical process that the novel traces through its consequences rather than its mechanisms.</p>

      <h2>The Timeline: 1876 to 1989</h2>

      <div class="sr-timeline">

        <div class="sr-timeline-entry">
          <div class="sr-timeline-year">1876</div>
          <div class="sr-timeline-event">Japan forces Korea to open its ports through an unequal treaty, beginning a half-century of growing Japanese dominance.</div>
        </div>

        <div class="sr-timeline-entry">
          <div class="sr-timeline-year">1905</div>
          <div class="sr-timeline-event">Following victory in the Russo-Japanese War, Japan compels Korea to become a protectorate, stripping it of independent foreign policy.</div>
        </div>

        <div class="sr-timeline-entry">
          <div class="sr-timeline-year">1910</div>
          <div class="sr-timeline-event">Japan formally annexes Korea. The Korea-Japan Annexation Treaty is signed not by the Korean emperor but by his prime minister, Yi Wan-yong, who becomes the most reviled collaborator in Korean history. Thirty-five years of colonial rule begin. <em>The novel starts here.</em></div>
        </div>

        <div class="sr-timeline-entry">
          <div class="sr-timeline-year">1919</div>
          <div class="sr-timeline-event">The March First Movement: an estimated two million Koreans participate in nationwide demonstrations for independence. Japan crushes the protests, killing thousands, and arrests Koreans — including Christian leaders — who organized resistance. Isak's older brother Sameol dies in connection with these events.</div>
        </div>

        <div class="sr-timeline-entry">
          <div class="sr-timeline-year">1923</div>
          <div class="sr-timeline-event">The Great Kantō Earthquake devastates Tokyo and Yokohama. In the chaos that follows, rumors circulate that Koreans have poisoned water supplies; Japanese vigilante groups and police massacre several thousand Koreans living in Japan.</div>
        </div>

        <div class="sr-timeline-entry">
          <div class="sr-timeline-year">1930s</div>
          <div class="sr-timeline-event">Agricultural crisis and colonial dispossession accelerate Korean emigration to Japan. Sunja and Isak arrive in Osaka in this decade, joining a community of several hundred thousand Koreans concentrated in the Ikaino neighborhood.</div>
        </div>

        <div class="sr-timeline-entry">
          <div class="sr-timeline-year">1940</div>
          <div class="sr-timeline-event">The <em>sōshi-kaimei</em> name-change policy requires all Koreans to register Japanese-style family names. Compliance is officially voluntary; in practice, those who refuse lose access to employment, schools, and services.</div>
        </div>

        <div class="sr-timeline-entry">
          <div class="sr-timeline-year">1945</div>
          <div class="sr-timeline-event">Japan surrenders. Colonial rule ends. Approximately two million Koreans are living in Japan; most eventually repatriate, but roughly 600,000 remain.</div>
        </div>

        <div class="sr-timeline-entry">
          <div class="sr-timeline-year">1950–1953</div>
          <div class="sr-timeline-event">The Korean War. The peninsula is devastated and divided; the armistice of 1953 establishes a ceasefire that has never become a formal peace. Return to Korea becomes still less viable for those who stayed in Japan.</div>
        </div>

        <div class="sr-timeline-entry">
          <div class="sr-timeline-year">1952</div>
          <div class="sr-timeline-event">The San Francisco Peace Treaty takes effect. Japan formally recognizes Korean independence — and simultaneously strips Zainichi Koreans of Japanese nationality, making them legally stateless overnight.</div>
        </div>

        <div class="sr-timeline-entry">
          <div class="sr-timeline-year">1965</div>
          <div class="sr-timeline-event">Japan and South Korea normalize relations. Zainichi Koreans affiliated with South Korea gain "special permanent residency" — a legal status that provides security but falls short of citizenship.</div>
        </div>

        <div class="sr-timeline-entry">
          <div class="sr-timeline-year">1980s</div>
          <div class="sr-timeline-event">Japan's "bubble economy": real estate and stock market speculation drive extraordinary prosperity. By the mid-1980s, Japan has one of the largest economies in the world. Noa's and Mozasu's adult lives unfold against this backdrop.</div>
        </div>

        <div class="sr-timeline-entry">
          <div class="sr-timeline-year">1989</div>
          <div class="sr-timeline-event">The novel's final sections. Japan is at the peak of its bubble. Solomon is working at a Japanese branch of a British bank in Tokyo. The bubble will collapse in 1991, but the novel ends before that.</div>
        </div>

      </div>

      <h2>Where the Novel Moves: Osaka and Tokyo</h2>

      <p>After Sunja and Isak arrive in Japan, the novel's geographic center shifts to Osaka — specifically to the Ikaino neighborhood, a working-class district in Osaka's Ikuno ward that became home to the largest concentration of Koreans in Japan.</p>

      <p><strong>Ikaino</strong> is not a neutral address. The name itself — meaning roughly "a field where pigs are raised" — encoded what Japanese society thought of the people living there. Korean migrants settled in Ikaino because discrimination in housing blocked them from living elsewhere. Japanese landlords refused to rent to Koreans, and the community built its life in the space that remained. Korean markets, churches, community organizations, and pachinko parlors all took root in Ikaino. It is where Sunja and Kyunghee sell kimchi from a cart, where Isak ministers and is eventually arrested, where Noa and Mozasu grow up.</p>

      <p>The novel's later sections move to <strong>Tokyo</strong>, where Solomon works in the 1989 chapters. Tokyo represents the generational distance from Ikaino: Solomon is trilingual, internationally educated, and operates in the world of global finance. The move from Ikaino to Tokyo across three generations is one of the novel's measures of what the Zainichi community built — and of how much remains unchanged regardless of what has been built.</p>

      <h2>The Novel's Four Generations</h2>

      <p><em>Pachinko</em> is organized around four generations of Sunja's family, each of which inhabits a different legal and historical moment.</p>

      <p><strong>Issei (first generation): Sunja and Isak.</strong> Born in Korea, arrived in Japan as adults. They are legally Japanese subjects under colonial rule, but socially and economically Korean and marginal. Korea is home; Japan is the place they work and survive.</p>

      <p><strong>Nisei (second generation): Noa and Mozasu.</strong> Born or raised primarily in Japan. They are Korean by heritage and legal status but have grown up in the only country they know. Each develops a different strategy for surviving a society that classifies them as foreign. Their contrasting choices — Noa's driven attempt to assimilate fully, Mozasu's practical embrace of the pachinko industry — constitute the novel's central pairing.</p>

      <p><strong>Sansei (third generation): Solomon.</strong> Educated in America, fluent in Korean, Japanese, and English, professionally mobile, and still unable to acquire what his father and grandmother could not achieve: full belonging in Japan. The novel's 1989 sections test whether a generation that has done everything right by the system's own standards can finally escape the condition that the system has assigned.</p>

      <h2>The Title</h2>

      <p>A pachinko machine is a vertical pinball device, played in large, loud parlors across Japan. Steel balls cascade down through a maze of pins; the player has minimal control over where they land. Winning is largely a matter of chance; the machine is calibrated to produce a few winners and many losers. It is technically a game of skill but functions as gambling, skirting Japanese law through a prize-exchange mechanism that has always operated in a legal gray area.</p>

      <p>Min Jin Lee has written that pachinko became "an organising metaphor" for the novel: "life is like trying to win a game which may have been designed to make the player lose." The Zainichi Korean community came to dominate the pachinko industry not by preference but by exclusion. Barred from public employment and most formal private-sector work, they entered an industry Japanese society disdained and stayed when others left. The parlor is a livelihood chosen from foreclosed alternatives, converted through decades of labor into genuine success, in a space the dominant culture refuses to respect.</p>

      <p>The name is not only a metaphor. It is a description of the conditions under which every character in the novel lives.</p>

    </div>

